Clause (a3) requires that a reader can tell data-flow-adjudicated flows from
call-reachability-only ones FROM THE EMITTED RECORD, and that "the scope of
data-flow coverage may not be left to assumption". Neither half held.

THE LABEL WAS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE FLOW. cmd_verify_claims builds one DDG for
the whole repo and then dispatches per language on `if ddg_edges:` -- a
repo-global truthiness test -- so every language ran through
propagate_taint_ddg whenever ANY language produced DDG edges. A JavaScript
function is never in ddg_symbols (JavaScript has no def/use extractor), so the
walk could not run and the finding still came out `ddg_mixed`, whose documented
meaning is "the walk ran and did not confirm". Reproduced end to end on two
repos whose JavaScript is byte-identical and which differ by one unrelated
util.py: `structural` without it, `ddg_mixed` with it. The label is now chosen
on whether the DDG actually covered the source function -- which is what the
field's own docstring has always said `structural` meant. No existing test
moved: all five that assert `ddg_mixed` already place the source function
inside ddg_symbols, which is exactly why nothing caught this.

NEW `dataflow_coverage` BLOCK, envelope schema 1.6 -> 1.7, additive, and
rendered on the TEXT surface too -- a disclosure that exists only under --json
is half shipped (WI-bifob's exclusion bucket reached the dataclass and never
the text renderer). One row per analyzed language with a taint catalog: the
catalog it would serve, and four independent capability bits (cfg_mapping,
atomic_statement, def_use_extractor, ddg_spec) with `dataflow_capable` their
conjunction and `blockers` naming the missing ones. Four bits rather than one
flag because each is INDIVIDUALLY sufficient to keep the machinery silently
inert: Rust shipped an extractor in March and emitted zero DDG edges for
months while failing three at once, and Java ships cfg_nodes/java.yaml while
declaring no atomic_statement and registering no extractor.

TWO CONSTANTS STATE WHAT NO PER-RUN MEASUREMENT CAN.
  inclusion_decided_by = "call_graph_reachability" -- ADR-0017 3a is
  confirm-only, so every reported flow INCLUDING every `ddg` one was included
  by call-graph BFS. Reading analysis_method == "ddg" as "inclusion decided by
  data flow" is the misreading INV-sadah exists for, made twice here already.
  coverage_granularity = "language" -- capability is NOT a per-function
  completeness claim; cfg_nodes/go.yaml self-documents that `if err := ...`
  initializers are invisible to def/use, so a capable language still holds
  functions the walk cannot see into. WI-joluk is the finer signal.
Both are declared constants with tests on them, so neither claim can quietly
outlive its truth (R16).

MEASURED WITH PRODUCTION LOADERS, NOT RE-DERIVED (L53): 553 sources, 1062
sinks, 12 sanitizers across 15 languages; 274 sinks (25.8%) sit in a
data-flow-capable language. The campaign plan recorded 244/23% -- rust's 30
joined when WI-tohuk landed atomic_statement, which is the drift a computed
table removes. TypeScript is fully capable and has ZERO sinks.

The def/use force-import list moved from cli._build_ddg_for_verify_claims to
dataflow_scope.ensure_def_use_extractors_registered: both registries populate
as an import side effect and the coverage table needs them at the same moment,
and a scope table computed against an empty registry reports every language
incapable without erroring. One home, not two; the wiring gate follows it. The
non-vacuity floor for that failure is mutation-tested -- deleting the
registration call turns `go` into def_use_extractor: false and the test fires.

ALSO: MYPY STRICT RATCHET RESTORED, 682 -> 672 (INV-zogud).

Found while pre-flighting: dev at ffbf8a5b93 produced 682 whole-tree strict
errors against a committed shrink-only baseline of 672, which ci-complete
treats as a merge gate. Verified as real drift rather than an instrument
delta -- the ratchet run at the baseline commit d229793409 reproduces 672
exactly, and local mypy is the pinned 2.1.0 the baseline records. Nine of the
ten came from this campaign's own taint PRs.

One was not cosmetic. propagate_taint_ddg binds `line` to an int in its
statement loop and rebinds it to edge.get("line") sixty lines later, so mypy
inferred int and reported the guard `isinstance(line, int) and line not in
sites` as "Left operand of and is always true" -- while the comment three
lines above states exactly why that guard exists ("a non-int member would
reach the sink_line > source_line comparison below as a TypeError"). Draining
that redundant-expr the obvious way deletes a live guard in security-relevant
code, which is the shape that once took the ratchet to a CLEAN 832 -> 789 on a
rewrite deleting 43 live guards. The same function reused `site` for both an
int call-line and a (dst, sink) tuple, making its deduplication membership
test read as a dead container check. Both fixed by giving the second binding
its own name; no runtime behaviour changes.

Three more were a real signature mismatch: _ddg_taint_reaches and
_use_site_terminates declared dict[..., frozenset[str]] for parameters every
production caller supplies as defaultdict(set). dict is INVARIANT in its value
type, so that is three genuine errors at the one call site that matters. Both
now declare Mapping[..., AbstractSet[str]], which is what they actually
require. The remaining two: go_def_use's _HANDLERS is typed as the callable it
holds rather than Any, and ddg_build's get_language call carries a documented
cast (fingerprint.py makes the identical call and carries the identical error
in the accepted baseline; left alone so this diff stays scoped to its own
drift). The baseline file is NOT raised -- a shrink-only baseline may only
ratchet down, and raising it to accept a breach ratifies the drift (L33).

INV-karud stays VIOLATED. (a3) is met; (a1) is Phase 5, (a2) stays vacuously
true while 3a is confirm-only, and (b) carries three filed limits. Closing on
one clause would be a partition dressed as a resolution (L14).

